    "title": "Northeastern State Parliament Calls for Trials of Gooja’adde Detainees",
    "original_title": "Northeastern State Parliament Demands Trials for Gooja’adde Detainees",
    "slug": "northeastern-state-parliament-calls-for-trials-of-gooja-adde-detainees",
    "summary": "On July 26, 2026, the Northeastern State parliament passed a resolution urging the federal government to prosecute soldiers and officers held since the 2023 Gooja’adde clashes, citing human rights allegations. The detainees have been held for nearly three years without trial, and the resolution marks the first formal legislative push for judicial action amid ongoing tensions between Somaliland and Northeastern State over the disputed Sool region.",
    "content": "The parliament of Somalia’s Northeastern State passed a resolution on July 26, 2026, demanding that the government bring to trial officers and soldiers who have been detained since the 2023 fighting in Gooja’adde. These individuals face allegations of human rights violations. The resolution represents the first official legislative step to compel judicial proceedings for detainees who have been held without trial for nearly three years.\n\nThe Gooja’adde clashes in 2023 involved armed confrontations between Northeastern State forces and Somaliland troops, centered on the contested Sool region. Dozens of soldiers were captured and detained in the aftermath. Since then, human rights organizations have raised concerns about the lack of due process for those in custody.\n\nLawmakers cited parliament’s constitutional oversight role in passing the resolution. They argued that prolonged detention without trial undermines the rule of law and could fuel further instability. The resolution calls on the federal government to expedite investigations and ensure fair trials.\n\nThe timing of the resolution is significant because it comes amid heightened tensions between Somaliland and the Northeastern administration over the disputed territory of Sool. The region has been a flashpoint for conflict, with both sides claiming sovereignty.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development underscores the fragile state of justice and governance in the country. Many diaspora members have family ties to the region and are affected by the ongoing disputes. The lack of trials for detainees raises questions about human rights protections and the effectiveness of legal institutions in Somalia.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/07/27/northeastern-state-parliament-demands-trials-for-goojaadde-detainees/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=northeastern-state-parliament-demands-trials-for-goojaadde-detainees",
